# Test: the PEER's Hold Time wins the negotiation when it is the smaller one.
#
# RFC requirement: RFC4271-4.2-1 positive -- ze holds the session on the hold
#   time the PEER proposed, proven by the byte-exact NOTIFICATION with Error Code
#   Hold Timer Expired (4) arriving inside the peer's 3 seconds rather than after
#   ze's own 180. RFC 4271 Section 4.2: "A BGP speaker MUST calculate the value
#   of the Hold Timer by using the smaller of its configured Hold Time and the
#   Hold Time received in the OPEN message."
#
# VALIDATES: session_negotiate takes min(peerHold, localHold) and passes it to
# timers.SetHoldTime (internal/component/bgp/reactor/session_negotiate.go).
#
# PREVENTS: the mirror returning. ze-peer copied the two Hold Time octets out of
# ze's own OPEN body, so the two advertised values were always EQUAL and the
# minimum was always ze's. RFC 4271 Section 4.2's min-selection had never been
# exercised by any session this suite has ever run: the branch was reached, and
# it could only ever choose the value it would have chosen with no branch at all.
#
# THE DISCRIMINATION. ze proposes 180 and the peer proposes 3. Under the mirror
# the peer would propose 180 too, the session would hold for 180 seconds, and the
# NOTIFICATION below would not arrive inside the 20 s budget: the file fails on
# the value rather than on the mechanism.
#
# THE PEER IS SILENT ON PURPOSE (option=silent), as in deadpeer-holddown.ci: a
# check peer answers every message with a KEEPALIVE, and each reply would reset
# ze's receive hold timer, so "the peer went quiet" is otherwise unexpressible.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=silent:value=true
option=timeout:value=20s
# 3 seconds, the RFC 4271 Section 4.2 floor, against ze's 180.
option=open:value=hold-time:seconds=3

# End-of-RIB from ze once the (empty) initial sync completes.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
# NOTIFICATION, Hold Timer Expired (4), subcode 0, no data: 21 octets. It can
# only arrive this soon if ze armed its hold timer on the PEER's 3 seconds.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0015030400
EOF_PEER

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65000
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		# 180 seconds, sixty times the peer's. Ze can only tear the session down
		# inside this test's budget by holding on the peer's number.
		timer {
			receive-hold-time 180
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=25s
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
